WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
We ask a lot of questions
about our diet.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
What’s the right way to
treat a chronic illness?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
Fight off a virus,
lose weight?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.640
The problem is, we get
a lot of different answers.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.920
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.400
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.680
Today we continue our series
on Ozempic with the question:

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.120
"Why do most users quit Ozempic
and what happens when you stop?”

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.120
People may not understand
the potential limitations

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.240
of the new class of Ozempic-like
anti-obesity medications,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.520
especially given the wide hype
these drugs are receiving.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.160
For example, the drugs
don’t work for everybody,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.320
and even when they do,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.080
weight loss stalls out
after a year.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.280
The drug insert included
in every package

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.185
of the high-dose Ozempic
used for weight loss shows

00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:52.920
after about a year,
weight loss stops,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.840
even if you keep injecting
yourself with the drug.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.680
Is it because you’ve plateaued
at a normal weight?

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.240
Sadly, no.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.400
They started out obese,
and they ended up obese.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.200
Why does the weight loss
stall out?

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.840
Because of what’s presumed
to be a near-exponential rise

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.720
in calorie intake,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.360
as the same amount of effort
to cut calories,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.320
whether through willpower,
drugs, or surgery,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.800
is met with
increasing resistance,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.760
as ongoing weight loss activates
your feedback-control circuit,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.800
stimulating your appetite.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.440
So, at first,
the massive GLP-1 activity

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.200
triggered by the drugs
lowers your appetite,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.520
but as you lose weight,
your body fights back,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.800
ratcheting your appetite
back up;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.400
so, the same amount
of massive GLP-1 stimulation

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.880
causes less
of an appetite suppression.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.520
Within 12 months,
this resistance,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.080
together with the decreased
total energy expenditure

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.640
from being lighter,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.280
matches the persistent effort
to cut calories,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.480
and with calories-in
matching calories-out,

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.776
weight loss plateaus.

00:01:55.776 --> 00:01:57.720
Here it is, graphically.
The same thing happens

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.840
whether you’re restricting
calories on a diet,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.440
injecting these fancy new
GLP-1 Ozempic-type drugs,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.200
or going under the knife
for bariatric surgery.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.200
Take high-dose Ozempic.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.840
After the three-month ramp up
in dose,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.400
people are eating nearly
a thousand fewer calories a day.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.440
No wonder they’re losing weight.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.400
But then, the body fights back,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.760
pushing against
the high GLP-1 signaling

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.280
and jacking their appetite
back up.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.800
Not all the way back up,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.960
because they’re still injecting
these drugs every week;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.280
so, they still appear to be
eating 400 fewer calories a day

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.040
than what they used to.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.440
So, if they’re eating less,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.080
why aren’t they continuing
to lose weight?

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.920
Because they’re expending
400 fewer calories

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.352
simply because they aren’t
dragging 30 extra pounds

00:02:45.352 --> 00:02:47.320
(13 kg) with them
wherever they go.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.800
What happens
if you stop the drug?

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.120
In this study, everyone was
started on high-dose Ozempic

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.200
for five months
and started to lose weight.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.480
Then, some were randomized
to be unknowingly switched

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.160
to placebo injections.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.280
Those who continued
taking the drug,

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.880
continued to lose weight,
until the plateau.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.720
Whereas, those who switched
to placebo

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.360
started to regain all the weight
they had lost.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.400
It’s the same with tirzepatide,
sold as Zepbound.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.160
As soon as people
stop taking the drug,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.920
the weight starts coming back,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.080
and any benefits
that had been achieved

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.120
start to evaporate.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.560
Check it out:
here you can see what happens

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.200
when people stop
taking the drug

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.440
after being on it
for more than a year.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.080
The pounds pile back on,

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.675
two thirds regained
within a year.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.240
Blood pressure came down
with the weight loss,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.160
but then climbed right back up

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.880
as if they never even took
the drug in the first place,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.880
and inflammation
and blood sugars

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.000
start creeping back up too.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.920
So, not only
does weight loss stop

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.400
as soon as you go off the drug,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.320
but the weight you had lost
before comes back.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.640
So, like in this study,
you may have started out obese,

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:57.920
and ended up obese,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.920
and to maintain your
less-but-still-obese status,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.160
you have to keep
taking these drugs,

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.400
presumably every day or week
for the rest of your life.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.200
So, these are forever drugs,

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.720
the golden goose
for Big Pharma,

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.480
drugs for lifestyle diseases

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.400
that you may have
to take lifelong,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.840
since you aren’t treating
the underlying cause,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.880
a fattening diet.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.200
As the popularity
of Ozempic-type drugs,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.600
like semaglutide, soars,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.520
what do we need to know

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.840
about their effect
on our weight?

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.320
Even these newfangled
anti-obesity medications

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.280
aren’t a magic bullet.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.760
The overall weight-loss benefits
are notable,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.240
but nonetheless limited.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.040
The best of them can produce
a 20 to 25 percent weight loss,

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.360
but they still leave most users
overweight or obese,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.160
only modestly less so,

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.440
and, thus, still facing
major health risks.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.520
And, of course,
the drugs are only effective

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.480
as long as they’re used.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.400
Not only does
the weight loss plateau,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.840
but years or, presumably,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.160
a lifetime of continuing
treatment is required

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.560
just to maintain
their initial benefits,

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.240
and most people
stop taking them

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.720
within months before even
reaching an effective dose.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.280
The manufacturers
recommend ramping up dosage

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.840
over four or five months,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.800
but based on 169,000
real-world patients

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.360
using GLP-1 drugs
for weight management,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.280
most individuals didn’t stay
on their prescribed treatment

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.600
for even three months,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.080
which suggests
it was unlikely

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.400
they were achieving clinically
meaningful weight loss.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.760
In fact, only about half take
the drug for even two months,

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.560
and 80 percent quit
by six months.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.800
Why don’t people stick with it?

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.920
One reason may be
that these drugs are costly.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.240
And costly is a bit
of an understatement.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.800
The costs of these drugs
are enormous,

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.040
like $1,000 a month
in perpetuity,

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.080
since any lost pounds
can pile back on

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.560
if you stop taking them.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.600
Wegovy, the high-dose Ozempic
used for weight loss,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.800
costs up to about
$1,350 dollars a month.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.640
So, that’s like paying $15,000
out-of-pocket every year.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.880
And some say healthy eating
is expensive?

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.680
You could have Food Is Medicine
programs deliver healthy food

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.400
to your door for ten times less.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.480
You could even have
fully prepared meals

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.960
delivered to your door
at a fraction of the cost

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.720
of the drug.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.280
Of course, the drugs
should get cheaper.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:21.120
There are hopes that competition
will lower prices,

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.120
but each GLP-1 drug is protected
by approximately 20 patents,

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.200
many up to 2040 or beyond;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.080
so, they probably won’t get
much cheaper anytime soon.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.560
But isn’t obesity itself
expensive?

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.280
Even factoring in
health benefits

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.480
and discounted prices,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.060
these drugs
may not be cost effective.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.680
In one analysis,
total annual health care costs

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.320
doubled for patients,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.520
going from about $13,000
per year

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.880
before starting the drug
to $26,000 after.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.440
Now, the benefits of weight loss
accrue over time,

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.080
but because lost pounds
are commonly regained

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.280
after stopping the drug,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.400
an additional vexing conundrum
is created for payers

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.880
like insurance companies.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.920
Why shell out all that money
to get people the drug,

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.840
if most are just
gonna stop taking it

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.480
and simply regain the weight?

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.080
Why are the drugs so expensive?

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.200
"There is no rational reason,
other than greed,"

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.040
according to Senator Sanders,

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.320
noting that Canadians
and Germans

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.800
are paying about 10 times less
for the same drugs.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.280
Wegovy, the high-dose Ozempic
used for weight loss,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.920
can sell for $1,300 a month
in the U.S.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.080
but less than a hundred
a month in the U.K.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.880
Here’s what
different countries pay

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.320
for the exact same dose
of Ozempic.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:42.240
The researchers even calculated
the "estimated minimum price,"

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.400
how much it could
be manufactured

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.480
and sold profitably for.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.800
So, for 40 dollars a month,

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.120
Novo Nordisk,
the makers of Ozempic,

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.760
could be pulling in
a 10 percent profit margin.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.560
But why make 10 percent profit

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:59.120
when you can make
more than 3,000% profit?

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.640
But don’t they need
those huge profit margins

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.720
to recoup research
and development?

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.760
I mean, it’s expensive
to develop drugs.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.920
Novo Nordisk spends
twice as much money

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.920
enriching its shareholders,

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:14.080
by doing things
like stock buybacks

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.080
to jack up stock prices,
than it does on all R&amp;D.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.480
In fact,
most big drug companies

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.640
spend more on these
kind of self-enrichment schemes

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.720
than actual research.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.120
Whatever the actual cost
of developing a drug,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:32.320
drug companies may spend
far more on marketing drugs

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.760
than developing them.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.800
For example, while gouging
patients $1,000 a month

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:38.520
for their drugs,

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.080
Novo Nordisk was spending
millions of dollars

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.240
wining and dining doctors
to prescribe more of them.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.120
Hundreds of thousands
of meals and snacks

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.760
served to promote drugs
like Ozempic.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.000
In fact, Novo Nordisk
was actually suspended

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.400
from a pharmaceutical industry
trade association

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.600
for failing to disclose
its sponsorship

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:57.800
of a training course
for healthcare professionals

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.480
that just so happened
to plug one of its GLP-1 drugs,

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:02.560
a violation bad enough

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.040
for the Royal College
of Physicians

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.280
to sever its ties
with the drug giant.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.200
Anyway, back to
the original question:

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.960
Why do so few people
stay on these drugs?

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.880
Well, people may start out
obese and end up obese.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.800
Is it worth paying $1,000
a month just to stay less obese?

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.840
You can see why people
might just cut their losses.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.720
But no, the study showed that
most people who quit these drugs

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.520
after just a few months
were not only insured,

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:34.000
they had their drugs
covered by their insurance.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.160
So, maybe the reason
most people quit

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.240
so soon after starting
is not due to their cost,

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.280
but rather their side effects.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.600
